# Branching and Deployment Strategy

> **Migration status (2026-05-08):** prod runs on `homeserver` (Dell Wyse 5070, single Docker stack) — see `deployment.mdc`. Lightsail kept reachable for rollback during burn-in. The 3-branch model below still applies; only the deploy mechanics changed (docker pull + recreate vs. Lightsail IP flip).

**Canonical reference:** `docs/BRANCHING_AND_DEPLOYMENT.md` (includes transition instructions and checklist; **may still describe pre-migration Lightsail flow** — defer to this rule + deployment.mdc for current process)

## Three Branches

- **`main`** — Development. Can be messy, broken, WIP. Nothing deploys from `main`.
- **`staging`** — Release candidate. Cherry-picks from `main`. Deployed to the staging stack (when dual-env is built; currently the staging hostname routes to the same stack as prod). Data refreshes will run here.
- **`prod`** — **Mirror of production. NEVER update unless the change is actually deployed to production.** Updated only in two cases: (1) promotion — fast-forward to match `staging` after staging is verified, (2) critical hotfix — fix deployed directly. The branch must always reflect exactly what's running.

## Separation of Concerns

Three independent operations — never conflate them:

- **Code deployment**: `docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web` on homeserver against the stack you're deploying to. No data refresh.
- **Data refresh**: weekly job that ingests new salary/bulletin data. Will run against the staging DB (when dual-env is built); intermediate state today is "still on Lightsail / not yet running on homeserver".
- **Promotion (staging → prod)**: when dual-env exists, this is either (a) deploying the staging-tested image tag to the prod stack, or (b) atomically swapping the postgres-data volumes (for DB-level promotion after a refresh). No more IP flips.

## Workflows (Summary)

**Feature:** `main` → cherry-pick to `staging` → deploy to staging stack → test → iterate. **Never to `prod`.**

**Promotion (post-dual-env):** verify staging → fast-forward `prod` from `staging` → `cd /opt/stack/visa_bulletin_prod && IMAGE_TAG=<tag> docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web` on homeserver.

**Hotfix (critical prod issue only):** fix on `main` → cherry-pick to `prod` → deploy to prod stack → cherry-pick to `staging`. **Only for crashes/5xx — not for features or non-critical fixes.**

**Data refresh (post-dual-env):** reset staging DB from prod → run refresh pipeline against staging → smoke staging → atomic flip (swap postgres-data volumes between prod and staging stacks). See `deployment.mdc` "Weekly DB Refresh Pattern".

## Local Worktree Layout

**The Cursor workspace always stays on `main`** so AI rules (`.cursor/rules/`) are always available. Separate git worktrees handle `staging` and `prod` branches.

```
~/cursor_projects/
  visa_bulletin/           ← main (Cursor workspace, always open here)
  visa_bulletin_staging/   ← staging worktree
  visa_bulletin_prod/      ← prod worktree
```

**Cherry-pick workflow (never leave main in the IDE):**
```bash
# Cherry-pick to staging
cd ~/cursor_projects/visa_bulletin_staging
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
git push origin staging

# Cherry-pick to prod (hotfix)
cd ~/cursor_projects/visa_bulletin_prod
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>
git push origin prod
```

**Why worktrees:** Checking out `staging` or `prod` in the main workspace loses `.cursor/rules/` (those files only exist on `main`). Worktrees keep each branch in its own directory so the IDE workspace never changes branches.

## Key Rules

- **Never scp files to servers.** All changes through git branches.
- **Never check out staging/prod in the main workspace.** Use worktrees.
- **`prod` branch = exact mirror of production.** NEVER cherry-pick features or non-critical fixes to it. Only promotion (fast-forward from staging after staging is verified) or critical hotfixes (crashes/5xx deployed directly to prod). If the code isn't running on the prod-serving stack, it doesn't belong on the `prod` branch.
- **`staging` branch = what's on staging.** View via `visa_bulletin_staging/` worktree. All features go through staging first.
- **Tags mark releases.** `v1.X.Y` on `staging` before promotion, then on `prod` after fast-forward.
- **Code deploy ≠ data refresh.** Code deploy = `docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web` (~30 s). Data refresh = weekly pipeline run on the staging DB followed by atomic flip (~30 min total).
- **Audit Docker before touching containers on prod.** See `AGENTS.md` and `.cursor/rules/deployment.mdc`. Never `docker-compose up/down` without knowing what's actually serving — `vb_web`, `vb_postgres`, `vb_redis`, `vb_nginx`, `vb_cloudflared` are the current prod containers.
- **No more IP flips.** Production runs on the homeserver as a single Compose stack; promotion happens via image-tag bump in `docker compose up -d` or postgres-data volume swap (when dual-env is built). The Lightsail aliases `prod_2Gb_vm`/`staging_2Gb_vm` only matter during burn-in rollback.

## Code Deployment (Quick Reference)

**Image-tag deploy on homeserver** (the current production model):
```bash
# Cherry-pick to staging worktree, push (CI builds image automatically)
cd ~/cursor_projects/visa_bulletin_staging && git cherry-pick <hash> && git push origin staging
# Wait for GHA to publish ghcr.io/vyakunin/visa_bulletin:staging-<sha>

# Once dual-env is built, deploy to staging stack first:
ssh homeserver.local "cd /opt/stack/visa_bulletin_staging && IMAGE_TAG=staging-<sha> docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web"
# Smoke-test https://staging.visa-bulletin.us/, then promote:
cd ~/cursor_projects/visa_bulletin_prod && git merge --ff-only staging && git push origin prod
ssh homeserver.local "cd /opt/stack/visa_bulletin_prod && IMAGE_TAG=<tag> docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web"

# Until dual-env exists, only the single-stack deploy applies:
ssh homeserver.local "cd /opt/stack/visa_bulletin && docker compose pull web && docker compose up -d web"
```
